- net

' In this file, identity is data. A name commits to a custody key. Custody
' signs a log of statements. Each statement gives the key that signs the pages
' of the name from that time. A reader that holds the founding and the log of
' a name examines the signatures. Thus the reader knows the key of the name,
' and it asks no registry.
'
' These are the rules, and they are pure:
' - what a name is
' - what follows what
' - what a reader keeps on its card for a peer
' - how a world that is not claimed becomes the world of a claimant
'
' There are two keys, and each key has different work. The live key signs
' pages and moments, and it is in the place where the world runs. The custody
' key signs only this log, and it is not kept on the host. Thus a robbed host
' or a dishonest host can sign for the name until custody gives a different
' key. But that host cannot take the name.
+ pass
  + founding
    ' This is what a name commits to:
    ' - the form of its identity
    ' - its first custody key
    ' - a number that its maker binds in with it (this number is an
    '   invitation, the hash of its first tree, or no value)
    : form=nat custody=nat dat=nat
  + form
    ' This is the only form that there is: one Ed25519 custody key, and a log
    ' of statements as this file reads them. A founding gives its form, and
    ' the name of the founding covers the form. Thus a different form is a
    ' different number and a different name. (A different form can have
    ' several custody keys or a signer of a different kind.) A different form
    ' is not a second way to read a name that exists.
    ^ nat
    1
  + name
    ' This function gives the name that a founding makes: the low 128 bits of
    ' the hash of the founding. This is the width that Mesa gives a name. Thus
    ' a name proves its first custody key.
    \ made=pass/founding
    ^ nat
    = said=[f=nat c=nat d=nat] [made.form made.custody made.dat]
    | mod (PinHash (pin said)) 340282366920938463463374607431768211456
  + statement
    ' This is one entry of the log of a name. The custody key that applies
    ' before the entry signs it. seq counts the entries from 1, and prev is
    ' the hash of the entry before it. (prev is 0 for the first entry.) Thus a
    ' log is one chain. The entry gives:
    ' - the key that signs from now (live)
    ' - the life and rift that this key signs under
    ' - the key that has custody from now
    : seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat
  + word
    ' This function gives what the signature of a statement is made over: the
    ' name and all that the statement gives, as a plain row.
    \ name=nat seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat
    ^ nat
    = said=[n=nat s=nat l=nat r=nat k=nat c=nat p=nat] [name seq life rift live custody prev]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + link
    ' This function gives the hash that the next statement connects to. It is
    ' the hash of this statement, and it includes the signature.
    \ s=pass/statement
    ^ nat
    = said=[s=nat l=nat r=nat k=nat c=nat p=nat g=nat] [s.seq s.life s.rift s.live s.custody s.prev s.sig]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + standing
    ' This is where a name stands after its log: the number and hash of the
    ' last statement, and what that statement gave.
    : seq=nat last=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat
  + born
    ' This function gives the standing of a name before its first statement.
    ' The name has custody and no live key.
    \ made=pass/founding
    ^ pass/standing
    | pass/standing 0 0 0 0 0 made.custody
  + state
    ' This function signs the statement that follows a standing. It signs with
    ' the seed of the custody key that applies.
    \ seed=nat name=nat at=pass/standing life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat
    ^ pass/statement
    = seq at.seq.inc
      sig (Ed25519Sign seed (pass/word name seq life rift live custody at.last))
    | pass/statement seq life rift live custody at.last sig
  + next
    ' This function gives the standing after a statement, when the statement
    ' follows. A statement follows when it has the next number, connects to
    ' the last statement, and has the signature of the custody that applies.
    ' No value goes back. The first life is 1. A new live key gets a new life.
    ' Thus a life identifies one key permanently.
    \ name=nat at=pass/standing s=pass/statement
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    ? !(eq s.seq at.seq.inc) (./err 'pass_gap)
    ? !(eq s.prev at.last) (./err 'pass_fork)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify at.custody (pass/word name s.seq s.life s.rift s.live s.custody s.prev) s.sig)) (./err 'pass_forged)
    ? (eq 0 s.life) (./err 'pass_back)
    ? (lt s.life at.life) (./err 'pass_back)
    ? (lt s.rift at.rift) (./err 'pass_back)
    ? (and (eq s.life at.life) !(eq s.live at.live)) (./err 'pass_key)
    | ./ok (pass/standing s.seq (pass/link s) s.life s.rift s.live s.custody)
  + read
    ' This function gives the standing of a name after a full log. It examines
    ' the log from the founding of the name.
    \ name=nat made=pass/founding log=row[pass/statement]
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    ? !(eq name (pass/name made)) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? !(eq made.form pass/form) (./err 'pass_form)
    = start=either[iota pass/standing] (./ok (pass/born made))
    | log.fold start
      \ acc=either[iota pass/standing] s=pass/statement
      ^ either[iota pass/standing]
      ? acc
       > (./err why) acc
       > (./ok at) (pass/next name at s)
  + forked
    ' This function tells if two statements both follow one standing and are
    ' different. These statements are proof that custody signed two futures. A
    ' reader keeps the two statements and follows neither of them.
    \ name=nat at=pass/standing a=pass/statement b=pass/statement
    ^ bool
    ? (equal[pass/statement] a b) .false
    ? (either/is_err (pass/next name at a)) .false
    | either/is_ok (pass/next name at b)
  + lives
    ' This function gives each live key that a log gave its name, with the
    ' oldest key first.
    \ log=row[pass/statement]
    ^ row[nat]
    = none=row[nat] []
    | log.fold none
      \ out=row[nat] s=pass/statement
      ^ row[nat]
      ? (out.any((\ seen=nat (eq seen s.live)))) out
      | out.snoc(s.live)
  + custody
    ' This function gives the custody seed that the secret of an invitation
    ' makes. Each one that holds the invitation holds custody. One that only
    ' runs the world does not hold custody.
    \ secret=nat
    ^ nat
    | Sha256 secret
  + minted
    : name=nat made=pass/founding first=pass/statement
  + mint
    ' This function makes a name for a world that is not claimed. Custody is
    ' the secret of the invitation. The first statement gives the live key to
    ' the one that runs the world until the world is claimed. The name is
    ' complete from this time. Other worlds can address it, publish to it and
    ' cite it. The name will not change when the world is claimed.
    \ secret=nat dat=nat live=nat
    ^ pass/minted
    = seed (pass/custody secret)
      made (pass/founding pass/form (Ed25519PublicKey seed) dat)
      name (pass/name made)
    | pass/minted name made (pass/state seed name (pass/born made) 1 0 live (Ed25519PublicKey seed))
  + claim
    ' This function makes a claim, and a claim is one statement. The function
    ' signs the statement with the secret of the invitation. The statement
    ' gives the custody key of the claimant and a new live key, under the next
    ' life. The one that ran the world before cannot make this statement.
    ' After the statement, no reader believes a signature from the key of that
    ' one for this name.
    \ secret=nat name=nat at=pass/standing custody=nat live=nat
    ^ pass/statement
    | pass/state (pass/custody secret) name at at.life.inc at.rift live custody
  + record
    ' This function gives a statement as a record.
    \ s=pass/statement
    ^ myth
    . (myth/sing ['seq] (pails/n s.seq))
      put(['life] (pails/n s.life))
      put(['rift] (pails/n s.rift))
      put(['live] (pails/n s.live))
      put(['custody] (pails/n s.custody))
      put(['prev] (pails/n s.prev))
      put(['sig] (pails/n s.sig))
  + of
    \ record=myth
    ^ pass/statement
    | pass/statement record.nat_at(['seq] 0) record.nat_at(['life] 0) record.nat_at(['rift] 0) record.nat_at(['live] 0) record.nat_at(['custody] 0) record.nat_at(['prev] 0) record.nat_at(['sig] 0)
  + at
    ' This function gives the path where a card keeps the log of a name. The
    ' card keeps the founding at <card>/pass and each statement below it, by
    ' number.
    \ card=path seq=nat
    ^ path
    = base=path card.snoc('pass)
    ? (eq 0 seq) base
    | base.snoc(($u seq))
  + kept
    \ name=path state=net/sight
    ^ maybe[myth]
    | (net/record name state)
  + number
    ' This function gives the name that a card is for. That name is the number
    ' of the world that the card is kept under. But for the card that a world
    ' has for itself (/<self>/io/mesa), the name is that world.
    \ card=path
    ^ nat
    ? (lt card.len 3) 0
    ? (eq card.len 3) (quip_val card.at(0))
    | quip_val card.at(3)
  + held
    ' This function gives the standing that a card records for its name. It
    ' gives none while the card holds no log.
    \ card=path state=net/sight
    ^ maybe[pass/standing]
    = none=maybe[pass/standing] .none
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept card state) none
    ? (eq 0 record.nat_at(['seq] 0)) none
    | ./some (pass/standing record.nat_at(['seq] 0) record.nat_at(['last] 0) record.nat_at(['life] 0) record.nat_at(['rift] 0) record.nat_at(['key] 0) record.nat_at(['custody] 0))
  + mark
    ' This function gives a standing as the slots that a card keeps. key is
    ' the live key. It is the key that landings are examined against
    ' (net/key).
    \ now=pass/standing
    ^ myth
    . (myth/sing ['seq] (pails/n now.seq))
      put(['last] (pails/n now.last))
      put(['life] (pails/n now.life))
      put(['rift] (pails/n now.rift))
      put(['key] (pails/n now.live))
      put(['custody] (pails/n now.custody))
  + meet
    ' This function is for the first contact with a name. It keeps the
    ' founding and the log of the name on the card for that name. It examines
    ' the log against the name that the card is kept under. Thus the name is
    ' the only part of what an introducer gives that the reader accepts
    ' without proof.
    \ card=path made=pass/founding log=row[pass/statement] state=net/sight
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    | pass/meet_at card made log "" 0 state
  + meet_at
    ' This function does the same as meet for a name that the reader meets
    ' through a scope. The scope tells where the name is reached, and this
    ' function keeps that address with the standing. The reader accepts the
    ' address from the scope without proof, and the address gives reach. The
    ' reader accepts no other value from the scope without proof. If there is
    ' no port, this function keeps no address.
    \ card=path made=pass/founding log=row[pass/statement] host=str port=nat state=net/sight
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ? (maybe/is_some (pass/held card state)) (./err 'pass_met)
    ? (eq 0 log.len) (./err 'pass_empty)
    ? (pass/read (pass/number card) made log)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok now)
      = born=myth (myth/sing ['custody] (pails/n made.custody)).put(['dat] (pails/n made.dat)).put(['form] (pails/n made.form))
        first=row[[p=path q=writ]] [[(pass/at card 0) (writ/make born)]]
        entries
          | log.fold first
            \ out=row[[p=path q=writ]] s=pass/statement
            ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
            | out.snoc([(pass/at card s.seq) (writ/make (pass/record s))])
        mark=myth (pass/mark now)
        placed=myth (? (eq 0 port) mark mark.put(['host] (pails/t host)).put(['port] (pails/n port)))
        stand=writ
          ? (maybe/is_some (pass/kept card state)) (writ/poke placed)
          | writ/make placed
        head=row[[p=path q=writ]] [[card stand]]
      | ./ok head.weld(entries)
  + before
    ' This function gives the standing of the kept log before one of its
    ' statements.
    \ card=path seq=nat state=net/sight
    ^ maybe[pass/standing]
    = none=maybe[pass/standing] .none
    ?> (./some born) (pass/kept (pass/at card 0) state) none
    = made (pass/founding born.nat_at(['form] 0) born.nat_at(['custody] 0) born.nat_at(['dat] 0))
      name (pass/number card)
    % k=nat 1
      at=pass/standing (pass/born made)
    ^ maybe[pass/standing]
    ? (ge k seq) (./some at)
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept (pass/at card k) state) none
    ? (pass/next name at (pass/of record))
     > (./err why) none
     > (./ok now) (_ k.inc now)
  + turn
    ' This function is for a statement that arrived for a name that has a kept
    ' log. If the statement follows, the card keeps it and moves to it. If the
    ' card already keeps the statement, the statement causes no change. If the
    ' statement is different from the kept statement at its number, and
    ' custody also signed it, it is a fork. The card keeps a fork beside the
    ' other statement as evidence, and the card records the fork. Then the
    ' reader does not believe the name until a decision is made.
    '
    ' A statement is its own proof. Thus the one that brought the statement is
    ' not important.
    \ card=path s=pass/statement state=net/sight
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ?> (./some at) (pass/held card state) (./err 'pass_unmet)
    = name (pass/number card)
    ? (gt s.seq at.seq.inc) (./err 'pass_gap)
    ? (eq s.seq at.seq.inc)
      ? (pass/next name at s)
       > (./err why) (./err why)
       > (./ok now)
        | ./ok [[(pass/at card s.seq) (writ/make (pass/record s))] [card (writ/poke (pass/mark now))]]
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept (pass/at card s.seq) state) (./err 'pass_known)
    = was (pass/of record)
    ? (equal[pass/statement] was s) (./err 'pass_known)
    ?> (./some then) (pass/before card s.seq state) (./err 'pass_known)
    ? !(pass/forked name then was s) (./err 'pass_forged)
    | ./ok [[(pass/at card s.seq).snoc('other) (writ/make (pass/record s))] [card (writ/poke (myth/sing ['fork] (pails/n s.seq)))]]
  + sent
    ' This function gives a statement as it is sent. It gives plain numbers,
    ' and they are the same bytes from any kernel.
    \ s=pass/statement
    ^ nat
    = plain=[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat] [s.seq s.life s.rift s.live s.custody s.prev s.sig]
    | PinBytes (pin plain)
  + got
    ' This function gives a statement from bytes that arrived. It gives none
    ' if the bytes are not a statement.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ maybe[pass/statement]
    = none=maybe[pass/statement] .none
      stored=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    ?> (./some held) stored none
    ?> (./some plain) (fit[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]] (unpin held)) none
    = [seq life rift live custody prev sig] plain
    | ./some (pass/statement seq life rift live custody prev sig)
  + birth
    ' This function gives the founding and the first statement of a name as
    ' one line of text. The line has the three numbers of the founding and the
    ' statement as it is sent, with commas between them. It is what a link
    ' tells about a name. A world that is prepared under a name is started
    ' with this line.
    \ made=pass/founding first=nat
    ^ str
    = parts=row[str] [(nat/show made.form) (nat/show made.custody) (nat/show made.dat) (nat/show first)]
    | str/join "," parts
  + birth_of
    ' This function gives the founding and the first statement that a line of
    ' text gives. It gives none if it cannot read one of the parts, or if the
    ' part for the statement is not a statement.
    \ text=str
    ^ maybe[[made=pass/founding first=pass/statement]]
    = none=maybe[[made=pass/founding first=pass/statement]] .none
      parts (str/split_byte 44 (str/trim_ascii text))
    ? !(eq parts.len 4) none
    ?> (./some form) (nat/parse parts.at(0)) none
    ?> (./some custody) (nat/parse parts.at(1)) none
    ?> (./some dat) (nat/parse parts.at(2)) none
    ?> (./some bytes) (nat/parse parts.at(3)) none
    ?> (./some first) (pass/got bytes) none
    | ./some [(pass/founding form custody dat) first]
  + introduction
    ' This is one world that a name brings before a second world. The name
    ' already has a standing in the second world. to is the world that the
    ' introduction is shown to, and by is the name that brings. name and made
    ' are the brought world and its founding. seq and last show how far its
    ' log went. host and port give the place where it is reached.
    '
    ' vouch is the signature of the custody of by. consent is the signature of
    ' the custody of the brought world, over the same words. Thus no world is
    ' brought before a second world if its custody does not agree. What is
    ' shown to one world is not proof to a different world.
    : to=nat by=nat name=nat made=pass/founding seq=nat last=nat host=str port=nat vouch=nat consent=nat
  + brought
    ' This function gives what the two signatures of an introduction are made
    ' over. It is ten numbers, and the word of a statement is seven numbers.
    ' Thus a signature made for one is not a signature of the other.
    \ to=nat by=nat name=nat made=pass/founding seq=nat last=nat host=str port=nat
    ^ nat
    = said=[t=nat b=nat n=nat f=nat c=nat d=nat s=nat l=nat h=nat p=nat] [to by name made.form made.custody made.dat seq last (str/to_atom host) port]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + introduce
    ' This function signs an introduction with the custody seed of the name
    ' that brings and the custody seed of the brought world. It signs the
    ' introduction at the standing of the log of the brought world.
    \ vouching=nat consenting=nat to=nat by=nat name=nat made=pass/founding at=pass/standing host=str port=nat
    ^ pass/introduction
    = words (pass/brought to by name made at.seq at.last host port)
    | pass/introduction to by name made at.seq at.last host port (Ed25519Sign vouching words) (Ed25519Sign consenting words)
  + introduced
    ' This function tells if an introduction is correct for the world that it
    ' was shown to. An introduction is correct when all of these are true:
    ' - It is addressed to that world.
    ' - The name is the name that its founding makes.
    ' - The custody of the name that brings signed it.
    ' - The custody that the brought world had when its log was as far as the
    '   words show also signed it.
    '
    ' voucher is the standing of the name that brings, on the card that the
    ' reader keeps for that name. log is the log of the brought world, as far
    ' as the reader has it. A log that does not go as far as the words show is
    ' short, and the reader waits for the rest of the log. The function gives
    ' the standing of the brought world after the full log.
    \ me=nat voucher=pass/standing log=row[pass/statement] i=pass/introduction
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    ? (pass/vouched me voucher i)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok words) (pass/consented log i words)
  + vouched
    ' This function examines the part of an introduction that a reader
    ' examines before it holds any of the log of the brought world. This part
    ' is the world that the introduction is for, the name, the form and the
    ' signature of the name that brings. The function gives the words that the
    ' two signatures are made over.
    \ me=nat voucher=pass/standing i=pass/introduction
    ^ either[iota nat]
    ? !(eq i.to me) (./err 'pass_other)
    ? !(eq i.name (pass/name i.made)) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? !(eq i.made.form pass/form) (./err 'pass_form)
    = words (pass/brought i.to i.by i.name i.made i.seq i.last i.host i.port)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify voucher.custody words i.vouch)) (./err 'pass_vouch)
    | ./ok words
  + consented
    ' This function examines the part of an introduction that must have the
    ' log. It finds the custody that the brought world had when its log was as
    ' far as the words show. It makes sure that this custody signed the words.
    \ log=row[pass/statement] i=pass/introduction words=nat
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    = start=either[iota pass/standing] (./ok (pass/born i.made))
      then
        | log.fold start
          \ acc=either[iota pass/standing] s=pass/statement
          ^ either[iota pass/standing]
          ? (gt s.seq i.seq) acc
          ? acc
           > (./err why) acc
           > (./ok at) (pass/next i.name at s)
    ?> (./ok at) then then
    ? !(and (eq at.seq i.seq) (eq at.last i.last)) (./err 'pass_short)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify at.custody words i.consent)) (./err 'pass_consent)
    | pass/read i.name i.made log
  + letter
    ' This function gives an introduction as it is sent. It gives plain
    ' numbers, and they are the same bytes from any kernel. An introduction is
    ' twelve numbers and a statement is seven numbers. Thus one is not read as
    ' the other.
    \ i=pass/introduction
    ^ nat
    = plain=[to=nat by=nat name=nat form=nat custody=nat dat=nat seq=nat last=nat host=nat port=nat vouch=nat consent=nat] [i.to i.by i.name i.made.form i.made.custody i.made.dat i.seq i.last (str/to_atom i.host) i.port i.vouch i.consent]
    | PinBytes (pin plain)
  + opened
    ' This function gives an introduction from bytes that arrived. It gives
    ' none if the bytes are not an introduction.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ maybe[pass/introduction]
    = none=maybe[pass/introduction] .none
      stored=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    ?> (./some held) stored none
    ?> (./some plain) (fit[[to=nat by=nat name=nat form=nat custody=nat dat=nat seq=nat last=nat host=nat port=nat vouch=nat consent=nat]] (unpin held)) none
    = [to by name form custody dat seq last host port vouch consent] plain
    | ./some (pass/introduction to by name (pass/founding form custody dat) seq last (str/from_atom host) port vouch consent)
  + founded
    ' This function gives the founding that a card keeps. It gives none while
    ' the card keeps no founding.
    \ card=path state=net/sight
    ^ maybe[pass/founding]
    = none=maybe[pass/founding] .none
    ?> (./some born) (pass/kept (pass/at card 0) state) none
    | ./some (pass/founding born.nat_at(['form] 0) born.nat_at(['custody] 0) born.nat_at(['dat] 0))
  + beside
    ' This function gives the card for a different name. The world that keeps
    ' this card also keeps that card.
    \ card=path name=nat
    ^ path
    [card.at(0) 'io 'mesa ($x name)]
  + log
    ' This function gives the statements that a card keeps, in order, as far
    ' as the card followed them.
    \ card=path state=net/sight
    ^ row[pass/statement]
    = none=row[pass/statement] []
    ?> (./some at) (pass/held card state) none
    % k=nat 1
      out=row[pass/statement] none
    ^ row[pass/statement]
    ? (gt k at.seq) out
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept (pass/at card k) state) out
    | _ k.inc out.snoc((pass/of record))
  + pending
    ' This function gives the introduction that a card keeps for the world
    ' that the card is kept under.
    \ card=path state=net/sight
    ^ maybe[pass/introduction]
    = none=maybe[pass/introduction] .none
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept card state) none
    = word record.nat_at(['letter] 0)
    ? (eq 0 word) none
    | pass/opened word
  + welcome
    ' This function is for an introduction that arrived for the world that a
    ' card is kept under. This world accepts the introduction when all of
    ' these are true:
    ' - The introduction is for this world.
    ' - The introduction identifies the world that its founding makes.
    ' - A name that this world holds a log for vouches for the introduction.
    '
    ' The name that vouches is the brought world, when that world brings
    ' itself, or it is a different name. (A world that brings itself is a
    ' claimed minted name that tells where it now runs.)
    '
    ' Then the card keeps the introduction as the record of the name that
    ' brought the world. It gives the card its address only when the log kept
    ' for the world goes as far as the words show and that custody signed.
    ' Until then, this world does not hear the brought world. A card that has
    ' an address accepts no other address from an introduction. A world that
    ' moves uses a different rule to tell that it moved.
    '
    ' It is still possible that a different name brings a world that this
    ' world already hears, to take a second place. Then, when the two
    ' signatures are correct, the card keeps the record of who brought the
    ' world under that name (the slot brought/<name>). The address of the card
    ' stays as it was.
    \ card=path i=pass/introduction state=net/sight
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ? (lt card.len 4) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? !(eq i.name (pass/number card)) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? (eq 0 i.seq) (./err 'pass_empty)
    = met (gt (net/held ['port] card state) 0)
    ? (and met (eq i.by i.name)) (./err 'pass_met)
    ? (and met (eq (pass/letter i) (net/held ['brought ($x i.by)] card state))) (./err 'pass_met)
    = voucher=maybe[pass/standing]
        ? (eq i.by i.name) (pass/held card state)
        | pass/held (pass/beside card i.by) state
    ?> (./some stands) voucher (./err 'pass_unmet)
    ? (pass/vouched (quip_val card.at(0)) stands i)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok words) (pass/seat card i words state)
  + seat
    ' This function gives the writes that welcome makes after an introduction
    ' is vouched for.
    \ card=path i=pass/introduction words=nat state=net/sight
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    = log (pass/log card state)
      word (pass/letter i)
      met (gt (net/held ['port] card state) 0)
      kept=myth (myth/sing ['letter] (pails/n word))
      by=myth (myth/sing ['brought ($x i.by)] (pails/n word))
      route=myth kept.put(['host] (pails/t i.host)).put(['port] (pails/n i.port)).put(['brought ($x i.by)] (pails/n word))
      keep=writ
        ? (maybe/is_some (pass/kept card state)) (writ/poke kept)
        | writ/make kept
      taken=myth (? met by route)
    ? (and met (lt log.len i.seq)) (./err 'pass_short)
    ? (lt log.len i.seq) (./ok [[card keep]])
    ? (pass/consented log i words)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok now) (./ok [[card (writ/poke taken)]])
  + follow
    ' This function is for a statement that arrived from a sender that this
    ' world does not hear. The statement is for the card kept under the name
    ' of that sender. If the card has a log, this function does what turn does
    ' with the statement. If the card has only an introduction, the first
    ' statement starts the log from the founding that the introduction gives,
    ' as meet does. A statement is its own proof, but it does not make this
    ' world hear the sender. The address comes only from the introduction.
    \ card=path s=pass/statement state=net/sight
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ? (maybe/is_some (pass/held card state)) (pass/turn card s state)
    ?> (./some i) (pass/pending card state) (./err 'pass_unmet)
    = first=row[pass/statement] [s]
    | pass/meet card i.made first state
  + origin
    ' This is what a founding binds when its maker tells what the world starts
    ' as. It binds the pack that the first tree is made from, at a version,
    ' and the world that made it. The first tree lists that world as its
    ' maker. A world that does not start as this is not the world that the
    ' name was made for.
    : pack=path version=nat maker=nat
  + binds
    ' This function gives the number that a founding has for a first tree.
    \ f=pass/origin
    ^ nat
    = said=[p=path v=nat m=nat] [f.pack f.version f.maker]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + begun
    ' This function tells if a founding is for this first tree.
    \ made=pass/founding f=pass/origin
    ^ bool
    | eq made.dat (pass/binds f)
  + grant
    ' This is permission to act at the door of a world. It is the hash of the
    ' secret of a session and the time when it stops. The custody of the name
    ' of the world signs it. A grant is examined against the custody that
    ' applies when the grant is used. Thus a grant made before custody moved
    ' has no effect after the move. As a result, one that held a world before
    ' its claim has no grant that opens the door of that world.
    : name=nat session=nat until=nat sig=nat
  + leave
    ' This function gives what the signature of a grant is made over. It is
    ' four numbers. The word of a statement is seven numbers, and the word of
    ' an introduction is ten numbers.
    \ name=nat session=nat until=nat
    ^ nat
    = said=[d=nat n=nat s=nat u=nat] [1 name session until]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + allow
    ' This function signs a grant with the custody seed of the name.
    \ seed=nat name=nat session=nat until=nat
    ^ pass/grant
    | pass/grant name session until (Ed25519Sign seed (pass/leave name session until))
  + allowed
    ' This function tells if a grant opens the door of this world now. A grant
    ' opens the door when it is for this world, it did not stop, and the
    ' custody that applies signed it. The function gives the session that the
    ' grant is for.
    \ name=nat at=pass/standing now=nat g=pass/grant
    ^ either[iota nat]
    ? !(eq g.name name) (./err 'pass_other)
    ? (ge now g.until) (./err 'pass_ended)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify at.custody (pass/leave g.name g.session g.until) g.sig)) (./err 'pass_forged)
    | ./ok g.session
  + attested
    ' This function tells if a record is what a name said under any key that
    ' its log gave it. It examines the record at the version that the
    ' statement gives. A third world that was shown the record, the statement
    ' and the log examines the record and asks no other world.
    \ log=row[pass/statement] name=path said=net/moment record=myth
    ^ bool
    | (pass/lives log).any((\ key=nat (net/attested key name said record)))
  + witness
    ' This is a record that its publisher signed, with what a stranger must
    ' have to examine it against only the name of the publisher. That is the
    ' founding that the name is made from, and the log that gives the name its
    ' keys. The name is as the publisher spells it, and said is the statement
    ' of the publisher on that version. All of it is plain values. Thus a
    ' record can contain it.
    : name=path said=net/moment record=myth made=pass/founding log=row[pass/statement]
  + speaker
    ' This function gives the world that said the record of a witness, when
    ' the witness is correct. A witness is correct when all of these are true:
    ' - The name is the name that its founding makes.
    ' - The log reads from that founding.
    ' - A key that the log gave the name signed this name, version and record.
    '
    ' The function gives 0 when the witness is not correct.
    \ w=pass/witness
    ^ nat
    = name (pass/name w.made)
      at=path w.name
    ? (eq 0 at.len) 0
    = first=path [at.at(0)]
      mine=path [($x name)]
    ? !(equal[str] (path_show first) (path_show mine)) 0
    ? (either/is_err (pass/read name w.made w.log)) 0
    ? !(pass/attested w.log at w.said w.record) 0
    name
  + witnessed
    ' This function gives a name that this world holds of a publisher, as a
    ' witness. This world must hold the statement of the publisher on the
    ' name, its founding and its log. If it does not, the function gives none.
    \ me=nat by=nat name=path state=net/sight
    ^ maybe[pass/witness]
    = none=maybe[pass/witness] .none
      card=path [($x me) 'io 'mesa ($x by)]
    ?> (./some item) (net/show name state) none
    ?> (./some made) (pass/founded card state) none
    | ./some (pass/witness item.name item.said (unpin item.record) made (pass/log card state))
  + carried
    \ w=pass/witness
    ^ [name=path sig=nat version=nat record=myth form=nat custody=nat dat=nat log=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]]
    = none=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]] []
      log
        | w.log.fold none
          \ out=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]] s=pass/statement
          ^ row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]
          | out.snoc([s.seq s.life s.rift s.live s.custody s.prev s.sig])
    [w.name w.said.sig w.said.version w.record w.made.form w.made.custody w.made.dat log]
  + uncarried
    \ plain=[name=path sig=nat version=nat record=myth form=nat custody=nat dat=nat log=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]]
    ^ pass/witness
    = [name sig version record form custody dat lines] plain
      none=row[pass/statement] []
      log
        | lines.fold none
          \ out=row[pass/statement] line=[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]
          ^ row[pass/statement]
          = [seq life rift live kept prev said] line
          | out.snoc((pass/statement seq life rift live kept prev said))
    | pass/witness name (net/moment sig version) record (pass/founding form custody dat) log
  + shown
    ' This function gives witnesses as bytes. The bytes contain only plain
    ' values. Thus they are the same bytes in each world, and a world that
    ' gets them reads them back (pass/unshown). A statement of a log is sent
    ' in the same way (pass/sent).
    \ proof=row[pass/witness]
    ^ nat
    = none=row[[name=path sig=nat version=nat record=myth form=nat custody=nat dat=nat log=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]]] []
      plain
        | proof.fold none
          \ out=row[[name=path sig=nat version=nat record=myth form=nat custody=nat dat=nat log=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]]] w=pass/witness
          ^ row[[name=path sig=nat version=nat record=myth form=nat custody=nat dat=nat log=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]]]
          | out.snoc((pass/carried w))
    | PinBytes (pin plain)
  + unshown
    ' This function gives witnesses from bytes that arrived. It gives none if
    ' the bytes are not witnesses. The function assumes no fact about the
    ' bytes.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ maybe[row[pass/witness]]
    = none=maybe[row[pass/witness]] .none
      empty=row[pass/witness] []
      stored=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    ?> (./some held) stored none
    ?> (./some plain) (fit[row[[name=path sig=nat version=nat record=myth form=nat custody=nat dat=nat log=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]]]] (unpin held)) none
    | ./some
      | plain.fold empty
        \ out=row[pass/witness] item=[name=path sig=nat version=nat record=myth form=nat custody=nat dat=nat log=row[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]]]
        ^ row[pass/witness]
        | out.snoc((pass/uncarried item))
